# Chroma library, modern Earth: THE WORLD ANSWERS, writer 1 (spec chroma-world/spheres/world-answers-v1.md, 10-19).
# Spheres rule, prot, care. Nothing reads these until the Engine's world_answers switch is on.
#   E4 the sphere asks (cast_want asked_help rung 2, asked_lead rung 3, asked_speak rung 4): one moment each, tier
#      everyday, {place} the place in that sphere, five one-colour ways and one cross cycle; yes ways carry
#      lever: loyalty or lead, bargains lever: voice or office, a no lever: exit or neglect; one or two foot: each.
#   F2 decisions for others (tier: decision, cast_want: decide): four a sphere, 40 options, each colour leads 8.
#      rule  the late hours of the square W B R G | the ruling on the old footpath U G B>W
#            who sits on the council's committees W U R | the bill on recalling officials G R>W U>B
#      prot  how the watch walks the town at night W U B G | who goes out with the rescue team W R B
#            the watch at the march W U R G>W | who oversees the watch U>W B G
#      care  who the clinic sees first W U B R | the days at the care home G R B>G
#            the food bank's open door W B G | the reform of care for the old W U R>G
# Syntax: header of build.py. Footprints: FOOTPRINTS.md.

# ================================================================ E4: rule

== the doorsteps before the crossing vote
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 20-90
calls: UG
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: community, work
horizon: months
roles: colleague, elder, regular
cast_want: asked_help
sphere: rule
earth: you are a familiar face at the town's committee: they ask you to give your evenings to knocking on doors before a vote
gap: 3-8
scene: {N} has been a familiar face at {place} for two years now. After the meeting {colleague} catches {N} by the coats: the council votes on the new road crossing in six weeks, and the committee wants to know what every street thinks before it does. Could {N} give two evenings a week to knocking on doors with a clipboard? It is cold work, and most people only want to talk about the bins.
scene W: If the committee is going to speak for the streets, someone has to ask every street, not only the loud ones.
scene U: Nobody actually knows what the town thinks about the crossing; {N} could be one of the few who finds out.
scene B: A season of doorsteps would put {Ns} face in front of half the ward, and the councillors would notice.
scene R: Six weeks of evenings with a clipboard sounds like a long, grey stretch, unless it can be made into something livelier.
scene G: These are {Ns} own streets, and the people behind the doors are the people {N} has always lived among.
- W | take two evenings a week and cover every street on the list, fairly | lever: loyalty | v: conformity, universalism | foot: place rule.counsel | chance: 80
- U | say yes, and draw up a short survey so the answers can be counted | lever: loyalty | v: achievement, self-direction | foot: place rule.voice | chance: 75
- B | agree, on condition the report names who gathered it | lever: voice | v: achievement, power | foot: person patron | chance: 70
- R | say no to the clipboard, and keep the evenings free | lever: exit | v: self-direction, hedonism | foot: means time+.06 | chance: 90
- G | take only the home streets, where every door already opens | lever: loyalty | v: benevolence, tradition | foot: person neighbour | chance: 85
- W>U | offer instead to keep the committee's record of every answer | lever: loyalty | v: conformity, achievement | foot: person colleague | chance: 80
- U>B | tally what each street wants and show the councillors where the votes lie | lever: voice | v: power, achievement | foot: lesson numbers move a council | chance: 65
- B>R | say yes to be polite, and let the clipboard gather dust | lever: neglect | v: hedonism, power | foot: door broke your word | chance: 90
- R>G | make a street party of it, and ask people their views over cake | lever: loyalty | v: stimulation, benevolence | foot: person friend | chance: 70
- G>W | go door to door with the oldest neighbour, who knows every family | lever: loyalty | v: tradition, universalism | foot: person elder | chance: 80
works: By the vote the committee has a stack of answers from every street, and the councillors read them aloud.
works: People {N} has passed for years start nodding to {N} in the shop, as if {N} had become a bit of the town's furniture.
fails: Half the doors stay shut, and the report that goes to the council is thinner than anyone hoped.
fails: The crossing vote goes the way it was always going to go, and {colleague} wonders aloud what the evenings were for.

== the chair of the planning committee
tier: everyday
stages: adult mature elder
age: 25-90
calls: WB
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: community, work
horizon: years
roles: colleague, elder, rival
cast_want: asked_lead
sphere: rule
earth: you are well known at the town's committee: its members ask you to chair the planning committee
gap: 3-8
scene: The chair of the planning committee at {place} is stepping down after eleven years, and {colleague} says the members have talked it over: they would like {N} to take it on. It means every second Tuesday, a stack of plans to read, and neighbours who will stop {N} in the street over every extension and every shopfront. Nobody else has said yes.
scene W: A committee that decides what the town may build owes everyone the same hearing, and someone has to hold it to that.
scene U: Plans are long and dull and full of things that matter, and most members only read the first page.
scene B: The chair of planning is where the town's quiet power sits; whoever holds it is owed a great many favours.
scene R: Every second Tuesday in a committee room, for years, is a weight; {N} would want to bring some air into it.
scene G: The committee has its old members and its old ways, and they would want the new chair to be one of them.
- W | take the chair, and run every meeting by the standing orders | lever: lead | lead: rules | v: conformity, universalism | foot: place rule.counsel | chance: 75
- U | take it, and read every plan in full before each meeting | lever: lead | lead: knowing | v: achievement, self-direction | foot: mark learned a skill | chance: 70
- B | accept, and ask for a seat on the town's budget board in return | lever: office | v: power, achievement | foot: person patron | chance: 60
- R | turn it down, and keep arguing from the public seats instead | lever: exit | v: self-direction, stimulation | foot: door turned down a chance | chance: 90
- G | agree to stand in for one year, until an old member can take it | lever: loyalty | v: tradition, security | foot: person elder | chance: 80
- W>B | take the chair, and make the committee's word carry weight with the council | lever: lead | lead: rules | v: power, conformity | foot: person rival | chance: 65
- U>R | propose a rotating chair instead, so new members take a turn | lever: voice | v: self-direction, universalism | foot: person colleague | chance: 65
- B>G | accept, and give the longest-serving members the seats of honour | lever: lead | lead: favours | v: power, tradition | foot: person friend | chance: 70
- R>W | say yes on one condition: every meeting opens to the public | lever: lead | lead: inspiring | v: universalism, stimulation | foot: place rule.voice | chance: 60
- G>U | decline, and put forward the quiet member who has read every plan for years | lever: exit | v: benevolence, tradition | foot: means time+.05 | chance: 85
works: Within a year the planning meetings at {place} run the way {N} wanted, and fewer neighbours shout in the street.
works: {colleague} tells the others it was the right choice, and says so where the old members can hear it.
fails: The first big plan splits the committee down the middle, and both halves blame the chair.
fails: {rival} lets it be known around town that the committee has never been slower.

== the hearing on the town's budget
tier: everyday
stages: adult mature elder
age: 30-90
calls: BR
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: community, work
horizon: months
roles: colleague, rival, neighbour
cast_want: asked_speak
sphere: rule
earth: you are one of the town committee's best-known names: the council wants you to speak for the town at a public hearing
gap: 3-8
scene: The town's budget is going before a public hearing in the county hall, and the cuts on the table would close two of the ward offices. The council wants one voice from {place} to stand up for the town's side, someone people know and will listen to. {colleague} brings the letter round: they want {N}. The hall will be full, there will be cameras, and whatever {N} says will be quoted back for years.
scene W: The town has an agreed position, worked out over months; speaking for it means speaking for everyone who worked it out.
scene U: The cuts rest on figures, and most of the people in that hall will not have read them.
scene B: A full hall and cameras: there will not be many better chances for {Ns} name to be known.
scene R: {N} has strong feelings about the ward offices, and a hall is where feelings can move people.
scene G: The wards that would lose their offices are the old streets; it is their people who should be heard.
- W | agree, and speak to the council's agreed position, word for word | lever: loyalty | v: conformity, security | foot: place rule.counsel | chance: 75
- U | agree, and build the speech on the figures nobody else has read | lever: lead | v: achievement | foot: mark learned a skill | chance: 70
- B | say yes, and make sure the papers know whose speech it is | lever: lead | v: power, achievement | foot: person rival | chance: 70
- R | speak, and say what is really felt, the agreed line or not | lever: voice | v: self-direction, stimulation | foot: door defied an authority | chance: 60
- G | decline, and ask someone from the wards losing their offices to speak | lever: exit | v: benevolence, tradition | foot: person neighbour | chance: 85
- W>R | agree, and bring three residents from the wards to speak alongside | lever: lead | v: universalism, benevolence | foot: place rule.voice | chance: 65
- U>G | write the speech for someone else to give, and sit in the gallery | lever: loyalty | v: security, achievement | foot: person colleague | chance: 80
- B>W | agree, in return for a promise that the wards get a vote on any cut | lever: voice | v: power, universalism | foot: door kept your word | chance: 55
- R>U | speak from the heart, then hand out the full figures at the door | lever: lead | v: stimulation, self-direction | foot: person friend | chance: 65
- G>B | say no, with thanks: this season belongs to home | lever: exit | v: tradition, security | foot: means time+.06 | chance: 95
works: The hearing ends with the ward offices still open, and people stop {N} for weeks to say they heard it.
works: The words from that afternoon end up in the paper, and the council asks {N} back the next time.
fails: The cuts go through anyway, and the cameras catch the moment the hall stops listening.
fails: {rival} gives a sharper speech straight after, and that is the one people repeat.

# ================================================================ E4: prot

== the flood weekends at the rescue post
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 20-80
calls: RG
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: community, health
horizon: months
roles: colleague, neighbour, elder
cast_want: asked_help
sphere: prot
earth: you volunteer with the rescue team: the river is high and they ask you to give your weekends until it goes down
gap: 3-8
scene: The river is high again this spring, and the volunteer rescue team at {place} is short of hands. {colleague} rings on a Thursday: would {N} give the weekends until the water goes down, filling sandbags, driving the van, sitting up with the radio overnight? It is cold, wet work, and it might be most weekends until summer.
scene W: A team only works if people turn up when they said they would, and the rota has holes in it.
scene U: Half the trouble on flood nights is not knowing where the water will go next.
scene B: The rescue team is well thought of in town, and a hard spring on it would be noticed.
scene R: There is something in {N} that wakes up at the thought of the boats out on a dark, rushing night.
scene G: The low streets flood every few years, and the people there have always turned out for each other.
- W | sign up for the full rota, every weekend until the water goes down | lever: loyalty | v: conformity, benevolence | foot: place prot.rescue | chance: 80
- U | say yes, and keep the radio log and the river readings | lever: loyalty | v: achievement, security | foot: mark learned a skill | chance: 75
- B | give one weekend in three, and ask for the first-aid course in return | lever: voice | v: achievement, self-direction | foot: person colleague | chance: 70
- R | skip the rota, but come out on the worst nights when the call goes up | lever: voice | v: stimulation | foot: mark took a wild risk | chance: 65
- G | turn out with the neighbours from the low street, as everyone there does | lever: loyalty | v: tradition, benevolence | foot: person neighbour | chance: 85
- W>G | drive round the old people on the low streets and check on each one | lever: loyalty | v: benevolence, universalism | foot: door helped someone in need | chance: 80
- U>W | decline the sandbags, but draw up the team a proper call-out list | lever: voice | v: self-direction, conformity | foot: lesson a good list saves hours | chance: 80
- B>U | say no: the weekends go to studying for the next step up | lever: exit | v: achievement | foot: means time+.06 | chance: 90
- R>B | join the boat crew, where the hardest work is and where it shows | lever: loyalty | v: stimulation, power | foot: person friend | chance: 65
- G>R | say not this year: the house and the garden need every weekend | lever: neglect | v: security, hedonism | foot: door turned down a chance | chance: 90
works: The water goes down in May, and the team's chief shakes {Ns} hand in front of everyone at the post.
works: One of the families on the low street brings round a cake with a note, and {N} keeps the note.
fails: The rota falls apart in the third week, and the worst night finds the post half empty.
fails: The cold gets into {Ns} chest, and the last weekends are spent coughing in a wet coat.

== the new volunteers need a trainer
tier: everyday
stages: adult mature elder
age: 25-80
calls: WU
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: work, community
horizon: years
roles: boss, colleague, mentor
cast_want: asked_lead
sphere: prot
earth: you are trusted in the watch's volunteer section: they ask you to train this year's new volunteers
gap: 3-8
scene: The watch's volunteer section at {place} has taken on eight new people this year, and {boss} wants someone who has been around long enough to train them: the rules, the radios, how to talk someone down on a bad night. {N} has the hours in and the trust of the old hands. It is two evenings a week, and the blame when a new one gets it wrong.
scene W: The new ones will do whatever they are shown, so what they are shown had better be right, and the same for all.
scene U: The manual says one thing and the streets teach another; {N} knows which one keeps people safe.
scene B: Whoever trains the new intake has a say in who rises, and that is worth something.
scene R: Two evenings a week in a classroom is the last thing {N} joined the watch for.
scene G: {N} was trained by {mentor}, walking the streets side by side, and that is how {N} learned what mattered.
- W | take it on, and teach the procedures exactly as the manual sets them | lever: lead | lead: rules | v: conformity, security | foot: place prot.watch | chance: 75
- U | take it on, and rewrite the course from what really happens on the streets | lever: lead | lead: knowing | v: achievement, self-direction | foot: mark learned a skill | chance: 65
- B | accept, if it comes with a rank and a say in who is taken on | lever: office | v: power | foot: person rival | chance: 60
- R | decline, and stay out on the streets where the work is | lever: exit | v: stimulation, self-direction | foot: door turned down a chance | chance: 90
- G | train them the old way, out walking the beat beside them | lever: lead | lead: custom | v: tradition, security | foot: person colleague | chance: 80
- W>U | agree, and set every recruit a fair test before they go out alone | lever: lead | lead: rules | v: conformity, achievement | foot: lesson a fair test protects everyone | chance: 70
- U>B | take it on, and pick out the two best for the quick road up | lever: lead | lead: knowing | v: achievement, power | foot: means time-.06 | chance: 65
- B>R | agree to one season only, and keep the night shifts | lever: voice | v: self-direction, power | foot: means freedom+.04 | chance: 75
- R>G | take them on as a crew, with suppers after the long shifts | lever: lead | lead: inspiring | v: benevolence, hedonism | foot: person friend | chance: 75
- G>W | say no, and put forward the old hand who has waited years for the job | lever: exit | v: benevolence, tradition | foot: person elder | chance: 85
works: By the end of the year the new ones are out on their own, and {boss} says they are the steadiest intake in a long while.
works: One of the recruits rings {N} months later, after a hard night, just to say the training held.
fails: Two of the eight drop out before spring, and {boss} asks pointedly what went wrong.
fails: A new volunteer makes a mess of a call that everyone hears about, and the trainer gets the questions.

