scene: For three years {N} has gone to the carers' group at {place}, the Tuesday evenings where people who look after someone at home sit with tea and say what they cannot say anywhere else. The woman who has run it since the start is moving away to be near her daughter, and at the last meeting she asked {N}, in front of everyone, whether {N} would take it on.
scene W: A group like this keeps people safe only if it is there every week, with the same rules, whoever turns up.
scene U: Half the questions asked on Tuesdays have answers that nobody in the room knows.
scene B: The group could be more than a room and a kettle, if someone used what it has.
scene R: Holding everyone's worries every Tuesday would be a lot, and {N} has worries of {Ns} own.
scene G: The group has its own ways, its chairs and its biscuits, and people come back for exactly that.
