earth: you are one of the clinic's best-known people: the council is deciding its funding and the clinic asks you to speak for it
gap: 3-8
scene: The council is deciding whether to keep funding the evening clinic at {place}, and the clinic wants someone the town knows to speak for it at the open session. {boss} asks {N}: a doctor would sound like asking for money, but {N} has been there for years and people listen. There are ten minutes on the agenda and a vote straight after.
scene W: The evening clinic is there for anyone who cannot come in the day; that is the case, and it is a fair one.
scene U: The council will vote on a line in a budget unless someone shows them what the line pays for.
scene B: Councillors care about their seats, and the clinic's patients are a great many votes.
scene R: Ten minutes is enough to make a room feel what one evening at the clinic is like.
scene G: The people who use the clinic are the town's own, and they should not need a stranger to speak for them.
