- G>W | say no this time: the season at home is too full | lever: neglect | v: security, tradition | foot: means time+.05 | chance: 95
works: The vote goes the clinic's way, and the evening doors stay open for another three years.
works: {boss} puts a copy of the council's minutes on the staff room wall, with {Ns} name in them.
fails: The council cuts the evening hours to two nights a week, and the waiting room fills on those two.
fails: {rival} stands up after and calls the clinic a luxury, and that is what the paper prints.

# ================================================================ F2: rule

== the late hours of the square
tier: decision
cast_want: decide
sphere: rule
stages: adult mature elder
age: 25-90
tone: mixed
life: community, work
horizon: years
roles: colleague, rival, regular
earth: the council must settle how late the square may trade and play, and {N}'s word now carries
scene: Every summer the square fills later and louder: the cafes want to stay open past midnight, the flats above want to sleep, and the market traders want their pitches clear by dawn. The council has argued about it for two years. Now the bylaw sits with {N}, and what {N} proposes is what the council will pass.
- W | one closing hour for every venue in the square, the same all year | v: conformity, universalism | scope: town_sphere | chance: 70
- B | late licences for any venue that applies and pays the fee | v: achievement, power | scope: town_sphere | chance: 65
- R | let the square stay open late on summer weekends, and see how it goes | v: hedonism, stimulation | scope: town_sphere | chance: 60
- G | keep the old market-day hours, written into the bylaw | v: tradition, security | scope: town_sphere | chance: 75
works: The square settles into its new rhythm within a season, and the letters to the council thin out.
works: {colleague} says it is the first bylaw in years that nobody has tried to overturn.
fails: The flats and the cafes both feel cheated, and the complaints keep coming to {Ns} door.
fails: {rival} gets the bylaw sent back for review, and the summer passes under the old muddle.

== the ruling on the old footpath
tier: decision
cast_want: decide
sphere: rule
stages: adult mature elder
age: 25-90
tone: mixed
life: community
horizon: years
roles: colleague, rival, elder
earth: a landowner has fenced an old footpath, and the panel {N} chairs must rule on it
scene: A landowner has fenced the footpath that runs along the old mill race, saying it was never a right of way. Half the town walked it as children; the other half has never heard of it. The dispute has come to the council's rights-of-way panel, which {N} now chairs, and the ruling will set the rule for every path like it.
- U | commission a survey of the old deeds, and rule by what they show | v: achievement, universalism | scope: town_sphere | chance: 70
- G | rule for the path as the town has always walked it | v: tradition, security | scope: town_sphere | chance: 70
- B>W | sell the strip to the landowner, and spend the money on the town's other paths | v: power, universalism | scope: town_sphere | chance: 60
works: The ruling holds, and within a year the path is simply part of how the town gets about.
works: {elder} writes to the paper to say the panel has done right by the town, and others agree.
fails: The landowner appeals, and the path stays fenced while the lawyers take their time.
fails: {rival} says the panel decided before it listened, and the panel's next ruling is watched closely.

== who sits on the council's committees
tier: decision
cast_want: decide
sphere: rule
stages: adult mature elder
age: 25-90
tone: mixed
life: community, work
horizon: years
roles: colleague, rival, boss
earth: the council asks {N} to propose a new way of filling its committees
scene: The council's committees have been filled the same way for as long as anyone remembers: the members pick each other. Turnout is low, the same names keep coming round, and the papers have started calling it a closed shop. {N} has been asked to put forward a new way, and the council will take whatever {N} brings.
- W | open every committee seat to election by the wards | v: universalism, conformity | scope: town_sphere | chance: 65
- U | appoint members by what they know of the matter in hand | v: achievement, self-direction | scope: town_sphere | chance: 70
- R | draw some members by lot from the residents, for one year at a time | v: universalism, stimulation | scope: town_sphere | chance: 55
works: The new committees meet for the first time with faces nobody has seen there before, and the papers notice.
works: {colleague} admits, after a year, that the meetings are better than they used to be.
fails: The first year is slow and quarrelsome, and the old members say they told everyone so.
fails: {rival} wins the council round to a review, and the change is put on hold.

== the bill on recalling officials
tier: decision
cast_want: decide
sphere: rule
stages: adult mature elder
age: 30-90
tone: mixed
life: community, work
horizon: years
roles: colleague, rival, boss
earth: a national bill would let residents recall their officials by petition, and {N} is asked where the town stands
scene: A bill in the national assembly would let residents remove any local official before the end of a term, if enough of them sign a petition. {Ns} name now carries beyond the town, and both sides want it. The minister's office has asked {N} to give evidence, and the town council wants to know which way {N} will go before it decides its own position.
- G | keep the town's charter as it stands: it has served for generations | v: tradition, security | scope: town_sphere | chance: 70
- R>W | back the bill: any official can be recalled if enough residents sign | v: self-direction, universalism | scope: nation | chance: 45
- U>B | propose a trial in this town first, with every result measured and published | v: achievement, self-direction | scope: town_sphere | chance: 60
works: {Ns} evidence is quoted in the assembly, and the town's own council follows {N} the same week.
works: The way {N} put it becomes the way people in town talk about the question.
fails: The assembly hears {N} out and does what it was going to do anyway.
fails: {rival} paints {Ns} evidence as one person's hobby horse, and some of it sticks.

